# Billr

Connect to hosted Billr at `https://mcp.billr.cc` using OAuth. A Billr
account and the access required by your plan are needed; installing this
skill does not create an account or include a subscription. Never ask for
a password or token in chat. For self-hosting, use the operator-provided
MCP URL instead, and confirm the intended service before authorization.

Billr is an invoicing and billing system for independent operators and small
studios. This skill covers how to use its MCP server well.

**The one rule.** You may read anything the connection is scoped to. You may
draft. You never put money in motion or put a document in front of a client
without the person asking you to, in the turn they ask. Sending an invoice or
an estimate, recording a payment, voiding a payment and deleting an entry are
all one-way doors for somebody else's money.

## Before the first call

1. Call `list_businesses`. A connection can be scoped to one business or
   several. If more than one comes back and the request does not name one,
   ask which before writing anything.
2. Call `get_business` for currency, tax settings and invoice numbering
   before you quote or draft a figure. Never assume a currency.
3. If a tool you expect is missing from the tool list, the connection was not
   granted that permission. Say so plainly instead of working around it.

`references/tools.md` is the full tool list with what each one does and what
it needs. Read it when you are not sure a tool exists; do not guess a name.

## Cards in ChatGPT and Codex

In ChatGPT, and in Codex inside the ChatGPT desktop app where it renders
them, four tools show a Billr card under your reply: `get_invoice`,
`get_estimate`, `revenue_summary` and `list_ready_to_bill`. When the person
asks to *see* an invoice, an estimate, their revenue or what is ready to bill,
call that tool rather than describing the data in prose; the card does the
showing, so keep your own text to a sentence.

The card's buttons never act on their own. Each one sends a message in the
person's name, such as "Send invoice INV-0042 to Harbor & Lane." Treat that
message exactly like a typed request: it is the person asking, in this turn,
so a send it names is allowed, but confirm the recipient if it is ambiguous
and never widen it beyond what the message says.

## Authentication and scopes

The distributed plugin connects to `https://mcp.billr.cc`. A self-hosted
operator may explicitly choose their own origin's `/api/mcp` endpoint.
There are two authentication paths:

- **OAuth (preferred).** The client only needs the URL. It discovers Billr's
  authorization server, registers itself, and sends the person to a consent
  screen where they choose the business and the permissions. Tokens expire
  after 30 days and refresh automatically.
- **API key.** A `billr_sk_` key, created in the app under Agents then API
  Keys, sent as `Authorization: Bearer <key>`. Shown once, expires after 90
  days, revocable at any time.

Five permissions exist: `read`, `write`, `send`, `delete`, `payments`. The
named profiles are:

| Profile | Permissions | What it can do |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Read only | `read` | Look at everything, change nothing |
| Read and draft | `read`, `write` | Also create and edit drafts, log time and expenses |
| Billing | `read`, `write`, `send`, `payments` | Also send documents and record payments |
| Full | all five | Also delete time entries and expenses |

Ask for the narrowest profile that does the job. Read and draft is the right
default for almost everything in this skill.

**Never write a key into a file.** Not into a config file you edit, not into a
committed `.mcp.json`, not into a scratch note, not into your reply. If a
client supports environment interpolation, use it (`${BILLR_API_KEY}`,
`bearer_token_env_var`, `${env:BILLR_API_KEY}`). Never print a key back, never
run a command that echoes one, and never paste one into a transcript. If you
see a key in a file the person is working in, tell them and stop.

## Safe defaults

- **Read before you write.** Every workflow below starts with a list or get
  call. Never create a client, an invoice or an estimate on the strength of a
  name in a sentence.
- **Draft, never send.** `create_invoice` and `create_estimate` produce
  drafts. Leave them that way. `send_invoice` and `send_estimate` put a
  document in a client's inbox; call them only when the person says to send,
  naming the document, in that turn.
- **Confirm amounts out loud.** Before any tool call that carries money, state
  the client, the amount and the currency, and quote back where the number
  came from: a saved item, a logged time entry, an estimate total. Never
  round, never re-price, never invent a rate.
- **Prefer server pricing.** When you have `time_entry_ids`, `expense_ids` or
  `engagement_entry_ids`, pass those and leave `line_items` out. The server
  prices the work and links the source records in one step, so nothing gets
  billed twice and nothing drifts from what was logged.
- **Use `request_id` properly.** Most write tools take a `request_id` UUID for
  idempotency. Generate a fresh one per intended operation, and reuse the same
  one, with identical input, when retrying a call that may have half landed.
  A new id on a retry bills twice.
- **One ambiguity, one question.** If a client name is ambiguous, a rate is
  not in the catalog, or a date is unclear, ask. Do not pick the most likely
  option and proceed.

## Core workflows

Full text of each is in `references/workflows.md`. In short:

- **Log billable time.** `log_time` against the client, with hours, date and a
  one-line description. Nothing else. Invoicing is a separate request.
- **Month-end invoice run.** `list_unbilled` per client, then `create_invoice`
  with the returned `time_entry_ids` and `expense_ids`. Every invoice stays a
  draft. Report a table and stop.
- **Overdue chase.** `list_invoices` filtered to overdue, `get_invoice_link`
  for the public URL, then write the follow-up emails into the reply. Never
  send them, never change invoice status.
- **Quote before you bill.** `list_clients`, then `list_items` to price from
  the saved rate catalog, then `create_estimate`. Stop at the draft. Later,
  on acceptance, `convert_estimate_to_invoice` (one bill) or
  `convert_estimate_to_engagement` (billed in parts). An estimate can become
  one or the other, never both.
- **Payment status.** `list_invoices` for sent and overdue, then
  `get_payment_status` per invoice rather than trusting the invoice total. If
  `mismatch` is true, stop, name the invoice, and report no paid figure for
  it. Read-only throughout.
- **Rate catalog upkeep.** `list_items`, propose additions and changes, apply
  only what was approved. Changing a saved rate changes what every future
  quote bills at.

## Task billing sessions

For agent work billed by active time and reported provider cost:

1. `list_billing_policies` reads the rates, multipliers, currencies and
   budgets a human already approved. **Agents cannot approve pricing.** If no
   policy fits, stop and say so.
2. `create_task` with a `policy_id` and a title. The task starts paused and
   carries its own budget.
3. `start_task` when active work begins.
4. `heartbeat_task` every 30 to 60 seconds while working. Only the first 120
   seconds after a heartbeat count, so a gap is unbilled time, not a gift.
   Retry a heartbeat with the same `request_id` so it is never counted twice.
5. `pause_task` whenever you are waiting on a person. Waiting is not work.
6. `record_task_usage` for each known provider cost, with the provider, a
   unique `provider_request_id`, the cost in the policy currency, and the
   `source` the figure came from. This is reported cost, not something Billr
   verified. Never estimate a price and never invent one.
7. `complete_task` once all usage is logged. It stops the clock and freezes
   the work for human billing review, and it cannot be undone.
8. `get_task_billing_preview` shows recorded active seconds and reported
   usage. It is a preview. Completion and a human's approval both come before
   anything is invoiced.

## Engagements

An engagement is an agreed total billed off a schedule: percent splits, equal
installments, milestones or manual entries. Engagements never send anything by
themselves.

- `list_ready_to_bill` is the "what should I invoice today" list across every
  client: deposits on started engagements, completed milestones, dated
  payments whose date has passed, final payments on completed engagements.
  Looking bills nothing and sends nothing.
- Bill an entry by passing its `engagement_entry_ids` to `create_invoice`. The
  server refuses to bill the same entry twice.
- `mark_milestone_complete` when a milestone is genuinely delivered, which is
  what makes it ready to bill. `waive_engagement_entry` drops an entry that
  will not be charged.
- `complete_engagement` closes the work: every entry waiting on completion
  becomes ready to bill, and the answer names any money left unbilled. It
  cannot be reopened, so only do it when the work really is done.

## Payment rules

- `record_payment` records money that has **already arrived** outside Billr: a
  bank transfer, a cheque, cash. Record only a payment the person has
  confirmed landed. Never record one because an invoice looks like it should
  have been paid, and never record one to make a balance tidy.
- Corrections go through `update_payment` (wrong date, method, reference,
  notes, or a mis-keyed amount) and `void_payment` (entered twice, entered
  against the wrong invoice, money that never arrived; a reason is required).
  The invoice is recomputed from its live payments, so a smaller amount
  reopens a balance. Do not record a negative or offsetting payment to undo
  something.
- A payment date in the wrong month puts the money in the wrong month of every
  revenue report. Fix the date with `update_payment` rather than voiding and
  re-recording.
- **Stripe money is refund-only.** Payments collected through Stripe cannot be
  edited or voided here. A card payment is changed by issuing a refund in
  Stripe, which arrives back in Billr as its own ledger entry. If a tool
  refuses for this reason, say so and point at Stripe; do not work around it.

## Error handling

- **429 with `Retry-After: 60`.** The per-key MCP limit is 120 requests per 60
  seconds. Back off for the stated interval and batch your reads; do not
  hammer a list tool per row.
- **`plan_required: ...`.** The hosted business has no active plan, so every
  write is refused while reads keep working. Report it and stop writing; the
  person fixes it in billing. This never happens on a self-hosted install.
- **"API key is expired, revoked or lacks permission for this tool."** The key
  was revoked or expired mid-session, or the connection never had that
  permission. Stop and tell the person to reconnect or grant the permission.
  Do not retry.
- **401 on connect.** The client has no valid token. For an OAuth client that
  means reconnecting; for a key it means the key is wrong, revoked or expired.
  A bad key gets a 401, never an empty list, so an empty result really means
  an empty account.
- **A generic "not found or not accessible" error.** Missing and
  out-of-scope records deliberately return the same error, so you cannot use
  it to learn whether something exists. Take it at face value.
- **A failed write you are unsure about.** Read before retrying, and retry
  with the original `request_id` and identical input.

## Things to never do

- Send an invoice or an estimate that was not asked for in that turn.
- Record, edit or void a payment that the person has not confirmed.
- Delete a time entry or an expense to "clean up".
- Invent a rate, a client, a date or a provider cost.
- Re-bill a `time_entry_id`, `expense_id` or `engagement_entry_id` already
  attached to a draft. Anything on a draft disappears from `list_unbilled`.
- Put a key, a client's contact details or a client's private notes into a
  reply, a commit, or a file.
